WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:10.760
Digital Forensics and Incident Response, usually shortened to DFIR, is the combined practice of understanding security events through evidence and helping an organization respond in a controlled way.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.900
The two halves are closely related.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.400
Digital forensics focuses on collecting, preserving, and analyzing data so that conclusions are reliable.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:28.140
Incident response focuses on organizing action during a security incident so teams can contain harm, restore operations, and make sound decisions under pressure.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.960
Forensics is not just looking at a suspicious file or reading a log.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.660
A good forensic process protects the integrity of evidence before drawing conclusions from it.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:45.280
That may mean preserving endpoint artifacts, exporting cloud audit records, collecting memory, documenting system state, and recording who handled each item.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.220
The point is to avoid turning a useful source of truth into a confusing pile of altered data.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.800
Incident response is the operational side of the same problem.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:02.740
When ransomware, account compromise, malware, insider misuse, or data exposure occurs, the organization needs more than analysis.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:12.560
It needs roles, communication channels, decision points, containment options, recovery plans, and a way to keep leadership informed without flooding everyone with uncertainty.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.740
Response turns investigation into coordinated action.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.520
DFIR matters because incidents rarely stay inside one technical lane.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:29.460
A single event can affect security, legal obligations, customer commitments, business operations, insurance questions, and executive decisions.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:38.080
DFIR gives the organization a disciplined way to answer what happened, what was affected, whether the threat is still active, and what must happen next.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.300
The goal is not drama or guesswork.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.000
The goal is evidence-based action that reduces harm and improves resilience.
